package main

// topics are the `fitness docs` topics, each the doc comments of the
// package whose doc comment names it with a //fitness:topic line.
var topics = []topic{
	{name: "config", pkg: "internal/conf", summary: "Package conf loads .fitnessrc.json — the Go runner's configuration file.", body: "Package conf loads .fitnessrc.json — the Go runner's configuration file. Same keys as the TypeScript FitnessConfig; JSON because the standard library parses it and a config file must not require a toolchain.\n\n## DefaultMaxListItems\n\nLoad reads root's config. A missing file returns (nil, nil): the caller falls back to the defaults. A config that still sets a retired exclusion key returns an error naming each one. DefaultMaxListItems is the longest list prose-budget allows, and the most bullets changelog-bullets allows in a release section, unless proseBudget.maxListItems sets another limit.\n\n## FileName\n\nFileName is the config file the runner reads at the repo root.\n\n## Config\n\nConfig is the shape of .fitnessrc.json.\n\nFields:\n\n  - policy: Policy selects org or external use; both default to all checks.\n  - checks: LegacyChecks optionally selects checks in external mode. Org ignores it.\n  - timeoutMs: TimeoutMs replaces the default per-check budget for checks that declare none of their own.\n  - localChecks: LocalChecks names a repo directory of the repo's own check programs, searched before the bundled ones. See ADR 0009.\n  - disabledChecks: DisabledChecks names checks external policy leaves out; org policy refuses it. See ADR 0009.\n  - repeatedStringLiterals: RepeatedStringLiterals holds options for that check.\n  - textReadability: TextReadability holds alarm bands for the text-readability check.\n  - proseBudget: ProseBudget holds the prose-budget check's hard caps. Each limit falls back to a built-in default when zero.\n  - comment: Comment holds comment-structure's budget for an issue or PR comment. Each limit falls back to a built-in default when zero.\n\n## LoadAs\n\nLoadAs reads root's config into a T, which holds the keys a caller owns, such as a language module's options. It returns (nil, nil) without a config file and fails as Load does.\n\n## MaxListItems\n\nMaxListItems is the repo's list limit: proseBudget.maxListItems when set, else DefaultMaxListItems.\n"},
	{name: "coverage", pkg: "internal/goal", summary: "Package goal holds the coverage goal every coverage check judges by, whichever language it measures: every counted line, branch or statement covered.", body: "Package goal holds the coverage goal every coverage check judges by, whichever language it measures: every counted line, branch or statement covered. A check compares exact counts, so a total that rounds to the goal still falls short.\n\n## Percent\n\nPercent is the coverage goal, as a percentage.\n\n## Met\n\nMet reports whether covered of total meets the goal. Nothing to cover meets it.\n\n## Text\n\nText is the goal as findings print it, such as \"100%\".\n"},
	{name: "github", pkg: "internal/metadata", summary: "Package metadata shares GitHub judgments between local checks and Actions.", body: "Package metadata shares GitHub judgments between local checks and Actions.\n\nIn GitHub Actions, `fitness pr-check` judges a pull request's title, body, closed issues and changelog link. `fitness issue-check` judges an issue by its kind label, and `fitness plan-check` judges Plan issues only. `fitness close-check` reopens an issue closed with unchecked items, `fitness dupe-check` notes an issue that reads like an older one, and `fitness comment-check` replies once to a comment that breaks the comment rules. Each reads the Actions event, runs the body checks on the text, and then its after-effect comments, labels or reopens.\n\nLocally, `fitness branch-check` runs the same judgments on this branch's open PR and the issues it closes, without posting anything, and the pre-push hook runs it before a push.\n\n## PushPlans\n\nPushPlans are the Plans one push read successfully, each read once. A failed read is not kept, so a later ask tries GitHub again.\n\n## NewPushPlans\n\nNewPushPlans starts a push with no Plans read.\n\n## LocalBranch\n\nLocalBranch judges live metadata through the normal check result protocol.\n\n## RunGH\n\nRunGH runs the named GitHub command against the Actions event.\n\n## WriteBody\n\nWriteBody saves body to a temp file for the --body-file checks; the caller removes it.\n"},
	{name: "hooks", pkg: "internal/hooks", summary: "Package hooks embeds the canonical may-journal git hooks that `fitness init` installs into a consumer repo.", body: "Package hooks embeds the canonical may-journal git hooks that `fitness init` installs into a consumer repo. The hooks call the installed `fitness` binary on PATH, so a repo pulls them by version rather than hand-copying them.\n\n## Read\n\nRead returns the embedded script for one hook name.\n"},
	{name: "languages", pkg: "internal/lang", summary: "Package lang defines what a language module brings to a run, and orders what the modules claim against what is installed or embedded.", body: "Package lang defines what a language module brings to a run, and orders what the modules claim against what is installed or embedded. A module names how to spot its language, the files it reads and writes, the tools it runs, its checks, its spell dictionaries, the words that name it as a technology, and how it proves a runtime. Everything no module claims is repo-wide, so a new repo-wide check or dictionary needs no list entry. The modules themselves live in the langs package, and each language's own code in a folder named for it beside that package.\n\n## Module\n\nModule is one language's entry in the registry.\n\nFields:\n\n  - Name: Name labels the module.\n  - Detect: Detect reports whether the repo at root uses the module's language.\n  - Exts: Exts are the source file endings the language owns.\n  - Manifests: Manifests are the file names that mark a project of the language.\n  - Lockfiles: Lockfiles are the file names its package managers write on install, each a name or a path.Match pattern for one.\n  - Wrappers: Wrappers are the names of the files its build tools' wrappers write, and a repo commits as written, to pin the tool's version.\n  - BuildOutput: BuildOutput, when set, lists the tracked files in a repo that are build output the language's projects write, which git must not track.\n  - Tools: Tools are the programs its checks run.\n  - Checks: Checks are the check names the module claims.\n  - Dicts: Dicts are the embedded spell dictionary files the module claims.\n  - Technology: Technology is the name the language's runtime products give their technology.\n  - TechnologyWords: TechnologyWords are the lowercase words an architecture container's technology string may use to name the language.\n  - Runtime: Runtime reads the language's manifests and sources for the runtimes a repo ships.\n  - Views: Views reads the language's sources for the architecture views: declarations and relations for the code view, and, when set, the references that prove the components view's edges.\n  - Inventory: Inventory are the source endings the architecture views list as the language's sources.\n  - Tests: Tests finds the language's requirement tests.\n  - Scans: Scans are the source endings a repo-wide check reads in the language, by check name.\n  - ToolConfig: ToolConfig, when set, reports a source file that configures a tool, such as a bundler config, rather than product code.\n  - TestFile: TestFile, when set, reports a file the language's test runner finds by its name or folder.\n\n## Testing\n\nTesting returns the modules that find requirement tests, in registry order.\n\n## Test\n\nTest is one test declaration and the acceptance ID its name carries, if any. Loc is file:line.\n\n## TestPrefix\n\nTestPrefix is how a module whose test names carry IDs names them: the prefix, then the acceptance ID with \\_ for its dot, so Test0001\\_1 or test0001\\_1 owns 0001.1.\n\n## Tests\n\nTests is how a module finds its requirement tests.\n\nFields:\n\n  - Scan: Scan, when set, reads the repo's tests and the files holding them, and reports whether the repo is written in the language. A module without it finds no requirement tests yet.\n  - Name: Name is the test name that owns an acceptance ID, such as Test0001\\_1.\n  - Source: Source, when set, reports a source path, as tracked, only tests build, which the architecture views class as a test. It gets the path's own case, since a language may tell folders apart by it.\n  - Numbers: Numbers, when set, lists the requirement numbers a test file's name carries, or none for a file not named for requirements.\n  - Tools: Tools, when set, judges how the repo's tests are written and run, given the files Scan found tests in. It returns one error per test file or run that uses a tool the language's requirement tests may not use.\n\n## BuildOutput\n\nBuildOutput returns the tracked build output files every module finds in the repo at root, in registry order.\n\n## Catalog\n\nCatalog orders the default checks: the installed checks no module claims and no ignore file names, sorted, then each module's claimed checks, sorted, in registry order.\n\n## Dicts\n\nDicts orders the dictionary files: the embedded files no module claims, sorted, then each module's claimed files, sorted, in registry order.\n\n## Exts\n\nExts returns every module's source endings, in registry order.\n\n## Ignored\n\nIgnored returns the names in the ignores folder, sorted.\n\n## Inventory\n\nInventory returns every module's inventory endings, in registry order.\n\n## IsLockfile\n\nIsLockfile returns a test, built once, of whether a file's base name is a lockfile a package manager writes, by any module's names or patterns or the lockfiles folder.\n\n## IsTestFile\n\nIsTestFile reports whether any module's test runner finds file by name.\n\n## IsTestSource\n\nIsTestSource reports whether a module classes the source path as built only for tests.\n\n## IsToolConfig\n\nIsToolConfig reports whether the module owning file's ending says it configures a tool.\n\n## IsToolWritten\n\nIsToolWritten returns a test, built once, of whether a file is one a tool writes and a repo commits as written: a lockfile, or a build wrapper file any module names. Its words and repeats are the tool's.\n\n## Lockfiles\n\nLockfiles returns every module's lockfile names, in registry order, then those of the ecosystems no module covers.\n\n## Runtimes\n\nRuntimes returns every module's runtime reader, in registry order.\n\n## ScanExts\n\nScanExts returns every module's source endings for the check, in registry order.\n\n## Sources\n\nSources maps each role to the endings of the languages no module covers that play it, in file name order.\n\n## TechnologyWords\n\nTechnologyWords maps each module's technology name to its words.\n\n## TestFileNumbers\n\nTestFileNumbers lists the requirement numbers a test file's name carries, from the first module that reads any.\n\n## Views\n\nViews returns every module's architecture view reader, in registry order, leaving out modules that read none.\n"},
	{name: "protocol", pkg: "internal/checkkit", summary: "Package checkkit is the contract between the fitness runner and a check binary, plus the main-function scaffolding every check builds on.", body: "Package checkkit is the contract between the fitness runner and a check binary, plus the main-function scaffolding every check builds on.\n\nProtocol: the runner execs `fitness-check-<name> --root <dir> [args…]` with cwd = root and context in FITNESS\\_\\* environment variables. The check prints exactly one JSON Result on stdout; anything meant for humans (banners, tool passthrough) goes to stderr. Exit code 0 when the check passed and 1 when it found problems. Exit 3 means the check could not judge (a panic or an internal error) and the Result carries that as info, which never fails a run. `--describe` prints a Describe JSON and exits without running.\n\n## ScopeFiles, ScopeBlob, ScopeGit, ScopeGitHub\n\nThe scopes a check declares: what it reads when it judges a repo.\n\n## BinPrefix\n\nBinPrefix starts the name every built-in check runs under, and every local check's file name.\n\n## ExitInternal\n\nExitInternal is the exit code of a check that could not judge the repo.\n\n## ScopeFileEnv\n\nScopeFileEnv names the file, one path per line, that lists the files under a `fitness --dir` folder. A folder can list more paths than one variable holds, so the runner hands them over in a file.\n\n## Check\n\nCheck is one check implementation: its metadata and run function.\n\nFields:\n\n  - Describe: Describe is the metadata --describe prints.\n  - Run: Run receives the repo root and passthrough args; the returned Result is emitted verbatim. An error return means the check could not judge: it is reported as info with exit 3, never as a finding.\n\n## Describe\n\nDescribe is the metadata a check prints for `--describe`.\n\nFields:\n\n  - name: Name is the check name, as `fitness <name>` runs it.\n  - summary: Summary says in one line what the check enforces. A built-in check leaves it empty: its package doc comment's first sentence is generated into the runner, which serves it here.\n  - scope: Scope is one of the Scope constants: what the check reads.\n  - timeoutMs: TimeoutMs is the check's execution budget; 0 means the runner default.\n  - contextInlineArg: ContextInlineArg names a flag (e.g. \"--message\") whose value the runner extracts from single-check argv into FITNESS\\_CTX\\_MESSAGE and strips from passthrough. Empty when the check takes no inline context.\n  - blob: Blob marks a check that judges a text given with `--body-file`, so `fitness --blob` runs it; the rest need files and are skipped there.\n\n## Outcome\n\nOutcome is what one tool run left behind. Code is the exit code, or 1 when the process ended without one, so a caller that only reads Code sees any failure. SpawnErr and Signaled tell a broken environment apart from a tool that ran and judged.\n\nFields:\n\n  - Code: Code is the exit code, or 1 when the process ended without one.\n  - Stdout: Stdout is what the tool printed on standard output.\n  - Stderr: Stderr is what the tool printed on standard error.\n  - SpawnErr: SpawnErr says why the tool could not start, nil when it did.\n  - Signaled: Signaled is true when a signal ended the tool.\n  - Err: Err is the error the run returned, nil on exit 0.\n\n## Exec\n\nExec runs cmd with stdout and stderr captured apart. A writer the caller already set on cmd.Stderr, such as os.Stderr for live output, still receives the stream.\n\n## ExecCombined\n\nExecCombined runs cmd with stdout and stderr in one buffer, in the order the tool wrote them, and returns it as Stdout.\n\n## Program\n\nProgram is how to start one check. Path is the file to exec. Name, when set, is the program name the check starts under: the one fitness executable runs the built-in check that name names.\n\n## Builtin\n\nBuiltin returns the program for a built-in check: the running fitness executable, started as fitness-check-\\<name>. Every check runs from that one file, so a new install loads one binary instead of one per check.\n\n## Self\n\nSelf returns the program that starts the running check again: this executable, under the name it was started with, since one fitness executable holds every check and the name picks which one runs.\n\n## Result\n\nResult is the single JSON object a check prints on stdout.\n\nFields:\n\n  - ok: Ok is true when the check found no problem.\n  - errors: Errors lists each problem found, one line each.\n  - filesChecked: FilesChecked counts the files judged; -1 when the check judged none.\n  - info: Info carries notes that never fail a run, such as an internal error.\n\n## Fail\n\nFail builds a failing Result.\n\n## Info\n\nInfo builds a passing Result that carries info notes.\n\n## Pass\n\nPass builds a passing Result.\n\n## Beside\n\nBeside maps the name of each fitness-check-\\<name> program beside the running fitness executable to its path. An installed bundle links every built-in check there to fitness itself; those links are the built-in checks, so they are left out. Any other program there adds a check, or replaces the built-in check of its name. None is no error, as after `go install`.\n\n## ChangedFiles\n\nChangedFiles returns the paths the runner scoped this run to: the staged files at commit time, a pull request's diff against its base, or the files under a `--dir` folder. Nil means the run is unscoped and every file is judged.\n\n## CtxMessage\n\nCtxMessage returns the context-inline commit message and whether the runner provided one at all (present-but-empty differs from absent).\n\n## DiffFiles\n\nDiffFiles returns the paths a commit or pull request changes. Nil means the run changes nothing, as in a full scan or a `--dir` folder, so a check that pairs changes, such as requirements, judges no pairs.\n\n## Document\n\nDocument sets the summary --describe prints when the check declares none. The runner calls it with the summary generated from the check's package doc comment before it starts a built-in check.\n\n## EnabledChecks\n\nEnabledChecks returns the enabled check names for this run, in order.\n\n## Internal\n\nInternal marks err as an internal error, reported as info with exit 3. A nil err stays nil.\n\n## IsInternal\n\nIsInternal reports whether err, or an error it wraps, is internal.\n\n## LastJSONLine\n\nLastJSONLine returns the last nonempty stdout line after any tool noise.\n\n## Main\n\nMain is the entry point every check binary delegates to: it parses --root/--describe, runs the check, and emits the protocol JSON.\n\n## SandboxText\n\nSandboxText returns the first sandbox phrase in output, matched without case, or empty when there is none.\n\n## StagedFiles\n\nStagedFiles returns the staged paths the runner provided (may be empty).\n\n## ToolMissing\n\nToolMissing is the internal error for a tool the check needs but cannot find.\n"},
}
